          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Nonabelian Hodge theory generalizes aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theory"} Hodge theory
            from abelian cohomology (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"}> abelian sheaf cohomology
            ) to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
          %h2#nonabelian_hodge_theorems Nonabelian Hodge theorems
          %h3#nonabelian_harmonic_sections Nonabelian harmonic sections
          %p
            Notice or recall (for instance from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+universal+bundle"} generalized universal bundle
            and
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated bundle
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            on an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            witnessed by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  V \to V//G \to \mathbf{B}G
          %p
            the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associated+bundle"} associated bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to X
            to a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \to X
            classified by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctor"} anafunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow} Y  \to \mathbf{B}G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi G
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  E &amp;\to&amp; V//G
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Since this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            diagram by definition, a glance at a pasting diagram of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi G
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; E &amp;\to&amp; V//G
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \nearrow &amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y &amp;\stackrel{=}{\to}&amp; Y &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
          %p
            shows that
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi E
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mrow
                            %mi σ
                          %mo ↗
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \&amp;&amp; E
                  \\\
                  \&amp; {}^{\sigma}\nearrow &amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y &amp;\stackrel{=}{\to}&amp; Y
                  }
          %p
            are in bijection with maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to V//G
            that make
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi G
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mo =
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Y &amp;\to&amp; V//G
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^= &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}G
                  }
          %p commute.
          %p
            In the special case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a connected
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a discrete group we can without restriction take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = \hat X//\pi_1(X)
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+groupoid"} action groupoid
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+cover"} universal cover
            by the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} homotopy group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to \mathbf{B}G
            is the same as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            homomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\rho : \pi_1(X) \to G
                  \\,.
          %p
            In that case the above says that a section of the associated bundle is a
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mover
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi : \hat X \to V
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the way these sections are formulated usually in the literature. The above description has the advantage that it works more generally in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            for
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
          %p
            Next consider furthermore the special case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V = G/K
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset"} coset
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homogeneous+space"} homogeneous space
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            quotiented by a subgroup
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+group"} algebraic group
            consider moreover a choice of
            = succeed "-invariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/K
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            structure on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p Then
          .num_defin
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong energy
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              of an associated
              = succeed "-bundle" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/K
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi d
                    %mi ϕ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    E(\phi) := \int_X |d \phi|^2
                    \\,.
          %p Here
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                is the
                = succeed "-equivariant" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ρ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
                is taken with respect to the chocen invariant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric"} metric
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/K
            %li
              %p
                and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral"} integral
                is taken with respect to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+metric"} Riemannian metric
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Such a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              is called
              %strong harmonic
              if it is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/critical+point"} critical point
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E(-)
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              %strong (Corlette, generalizing Eells-Sampson)
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho : \pi_1(X) \to G
              is a representation with
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                  a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reductive+group"} reductive
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+group"} algebraic group
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+compact+subgroup"} maximal compact subgroup
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ρ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho(\pi_1(X))
                  is
                %ul
                  %li
                    %p
                      Zariski-dense in
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi G
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                  %li
                    %p or its Zariski-closure is itself reductive
            %p
              then there exists a
              %em harmonic
              section
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              in the above sense.
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Corlette88"}> Corlett 88
            ). A version of the proof is reproduced in
            %a{:href => "#Simpson96"} Simpson 96, p. 8
          %h3#LocalSystemsAndHiggsBundles Kähler case: Equivalence between Local systems and Higgs bundles
          %p
            The
            %em nonabelian Hodge theorem
            due to (
            %a{:href => "#Simpson92"}> Simpson 92
            ) establishes, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            between (irreducible)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+vector+bundles"} flat vector bundles
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and (stable)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+bundles"} Higgs bundles
            with vanishing
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
          %h4#relation_to_the_abelian_hodge_theorem Relation to the abelian Hodge theorem
          %p
            The sense in which the nonabelian Hodge theorem of (
            %a{:href => "#Simpson92"}> Simpson 92
            ) generalizes the abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theorem"} Hodge theorem
            is the following (
            %a{:href => "#Simpson92"}> Simpson 92, Introduction
            ).
          %p
            The abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi disc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^1(X,\mathbb{C}_{disc})
            classifies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+vector+bundle"} flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+line+bundles"} complex line bundles
            whose underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            is trivial, hence closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-forms"} differential 1-forms
            modulo 0-forms. The abelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+theorem"} Hodge theorem
            gives for this hence the decomposition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi disc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H^1(X,\mathbb{C}_{disc})
                  \\simeq
                  H^1(X, \mathcal{O}_X) \oplus H^0(X, \Omega^1_X)
                  \\,.
          %p
            It is this kind of relation which is generalized by the nonabelian Hodge theorem. Here one starts instead with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi GL
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi disc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^1(X, GL_n(\mathbb{C})_{disc})
            which classifies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+vector+bundle"} flat
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rank"} rank
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+bundles"} Higgs bundles
            gives now a decomposition of these structures into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+vector+bundle"} holomorphic vector bundle
            classified by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi GL
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^1(X, GL_n(\mathcal{O}_X))
            and a differential 1-form with values in endomorphisms of that, subject to some conditions.
          %h4#statement Statement
          %p
            A quick review of the theorem in (
            %a{:href => "#Simpson92"}> Simpson 92
            ) is for instance in (
            %a{:href => "#Raboso15"}> Raboso 15, section 1.2
            ). An elegant abstract reformulation in terms of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohesion"} differential cohesion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-geometry"} D-geometry
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Simpson96"} Simpson 96
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Raboso15"} Raboso 15, section 3.3
          %p
            Analogous to how the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+stack"} de Rham stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi inf
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi dR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \int_{inf} X = X_{dR}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+quotient"}> homotopy
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            by the first order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times X
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi Dol
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{Dol}
            which is the formal completion of the 0-section of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (
            %a{:href => "#Simpson96"}> Simpson 96
            ).
          %p
            Now a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+vector+bundle"} flat vector bundle
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is essentially just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+stack"} de Rham stack
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi dR
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{dR}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+bundle"} Higgs bundle
            is essentially just a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mi Dol
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_{Dol}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a linear
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+group"} algebraic group
            over
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi dR
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi Dol
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi ss
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{H}(X_{dR}, \mathbf{B}G)
                  \\simeq
                  \\mathbf{H}(X_{Dol}, \mathbf{B}G)^{ss,0}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the superscript on the right denotes restriction to semistable Higgs bundles with vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Raboso15"} Raboso 15, Theorem 3.3
          %h4#generalizations_to_twisted_bundles Generalizations to twisted bundles
          %p
            A generalization of the nonabelian Hodge theorem of (
            %a{:href => "#Simpson92"}> Simpson 92
            ) to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+bundles"} twisted bundles
            in discussed in (
            %a{:href => "#Raboso15"}> Raboso 15
            ).
          %h2#relation_to_geometric_langlands Relation to geometric Langlands
          %p
            Nonabelian Hodge theory is closely related to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+Langlands+correspondence"} geometric Langlands correspondence
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+structure"} Hodge structure
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+Hodge+theory"} noncommutative Hodge theory
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              wikipedia
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonabelian_Hodge_correspondence"} nonabelian Hodge correspondence
          %p Lecture notes on nonabelian Hodge theory include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ron+Donagi"}> Ron Donagi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tony+Pantev"} Tony Pantev
                %em Lectures on the geometric Langlands
              %p
                conjecture and non-abelian Hodge theory_, 2009 (
                %a{:href => "http://www.icmat.es/seminarios/langlands/school/handouts/pantev.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Garc%C3%ADa+Raboso"}> Alberto García Raboso
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Rayan"} Steven Rayan
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to Nonabelian Hodge Theory: flat connections, Higgs bundles, and complex variations of Hodge structure
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.1693"} arXiv
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4939-2830-9_5"} Springer
          %p Corlette’s nonabelian Hodge theorem can be found in:
          %ul
            %li#Corlette88
              K. Corlette,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Flat
                  = succeed "-bundles" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi G
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            Works by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"} Carlos Simpson
            on nonabelian Hodge theory include:
          %ul
            %li#Simpson92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higgs bundles and local systems
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item?id=PMIHES_1992__75__5_0"} numdam
            %li#Simpson96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Hodge filtration on nonabelian cohomology
              %p
                1492538 (99g:14028) (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/alg-geom/9604005"}> arXiv:9604005
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
                ,
                %em Secondary Kodaira-Spencer classes and nonabelian Dolbeault cohomology
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/alg-geom/9712020"}> arXiv:9712020
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
                ,
                %em Algebraic aspects of higher nonabelian Hodge theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/9902067"}> arXiv:9902067
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Carlos+Simpson"}> Carlos Simpson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tony+Pantev"} Tony Pantev
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ludmil+Katzarkov"} Ludmil Katzarkov
                %em Nonabelian mixed Hodge structures
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0006213"}> arXiv
                )
          %p
            The nonabelian Hodge theorem of (
            %a{:href => "#Simpson92"}> Simpson 92
            ) is generalized to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+bundles"} twisted bundles
            in:
          %ul
            %li#Raboso15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Garc%C3%ADa+Raboso"}> Alberto García Raboso
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A twisted nonabelian Hodge correspondence
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1501.05872"} arXiv:1501.05872
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.toronto.edu/agraboso/files/TwistedNAHT_Talk_Handout.pdf"} pdf slides
          %p Gothen’s paper starts with a survey on nonabelian Hodge correspondence,
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Peter B. Gothen,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higgs bundles and the real symplectic group
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.4175"} arXiv:1102.4175
            %li
              %p
                C. C. Liu, S. Rayan, Y. Tanaka,
                %em The Kapustin–Witten equations and nonabelian Hodge theory
                Eur. J. Math.
                %strong 8
                (Suppl 1) 23–41 (2022)
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s40879-022-00538-4"} doi
        .revisedby
          %p
            Last revised on October  1, 2023 at 12:46:32.
